% M7-T12 / D-0077: an ASSIGNMENT decision taken UNDER another one, over a SATISFIABLE
% model. This is the lane that tests the assignment clause's TAIL.
%
% `median_assign_sat.fzn` takes its assignments at the root, where the clause a failed
% branch is weakened into has no tail at all -- `x <= 1 \/ x >= 3` is globally valid on
% its own. That is the easy half. Here the second assignment sits inside the first, so
% its clause carries the outer assignment's two literals, and the hole's own trace line
% carries them in its tail. A version of this row that punched the hole and stated only
% `u <> 1` would be D-0075's defect exactly -- a line that is not merely underived but
% FALSE, since u = 1 is perfectly fine for other values of x -- and this model sees it.
%
% The shape. Nothing is fixed by root propagation; every exclusion below is an
% `int_lin_ne`, which is VALUE consistent and therefore prunes only once one variable is
% left. So the contradictions have to be found by branching, which is the point.
%
%   x over 0..3 (sum <= 3 caps it), lower median 1, strictly interior -> ASSIGN x = 1
%   u over 0..2, lower median 1, strictly interior -> ASSIGN u = 1, at level 2
%   v is then forced empty (v <= 1, and v = 0 and v = 1 are both excluded) -> the branch
%     fails, and the clause `u <= 0 \/ u >= 2 \/ x <= 0 \/ x >= 2` is emitted AT LEVEL 2
%     with the outer assignment's two literals as its tail
%   u = 1 is removed and the node is RE-ENTERED; u's domain is now {0, 2}, whose lower
%     median 0 IS the lower bound, so the value choice returns an ordinary Split and no
%     assignment machinery is used
%   u = 0 gives v = 0 and the sum 1, which nothing excludes
%
% So one model covers both arms of the median dispatch and both halves of the
% assignment: a clause with a tail, and a re-entry that finds the answer.
%
% Declared domains are four and three values wide.
var 0..4: x :: output_var;
var 0..2: u :: output_var;
var 0..2: v :: output_var;
constraint int_lin_le([1, 1, 1], [x, u, v], 3);
constraint int_lin_ne([1, 1, 1], [x, u, v], 2);
constraint int_lin_ne([1, 1, 1], [x, u, v], 3);
solve :: int_search([x, u, v], input_order, indomain_median, complete) satisfy;
